Subject: RFI-007: Unexpected hard rock encountered at footing F-18 excavation; please advise
Date: Tue, 22 Apr 2025 10:42:18 +0530

Hi Dr. Sundar,

RFI-007. Please respond by EOD today if possible — we are holding F-18 excavation.

DESCRIPTION:
During foundation excavation for footing F-18 (Grid 5-D), the contractor has encountered **hard rock at a depth of 2.4m** (vs the expected 3.8m as per the geotech report). The rock appears to be sound granite (not weathered gneiss as expected).

ISSUES:
(i) Should we continue excavation to the originally specified depth of 3.8m, given the hard rock found at 2.4m would require blasting/breaking (cost + schedule impact)?

(ii) Alternatively, can we found the footing at 2.4m depth on the hard rock, with appropriate strip footing redesign (since SBC on sound granite is much higher than the design value of 480 kN/m²)?

(iii) Cost implication of breaking rock (option 1): ~INR 6 lakh + 3-day delay
    Cost implication of redesign (option 2): ~INR 1 lakh + 1-day delay (if approved quickly)

ATTACHMENTS:
- F-18 photo showing rock outcrop (jpg)
- Site survey notes (PDF)

Please advise the preferred approach. Acme team — copying you for awareness; this is a small contingency we are handling within budget tolerance.

